  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nThrough Tuesday: Excessive heat will remain the chief focus \nthrough tomorrow as we remain on the northern periphery of a \nsurface high over the Gulf. Heat indices peaking above 105F will \nbe common areawide, with readings in the 110-115F range possible. \nA combination of Heat Advisory and Extreme Heat Warning will \nremain valid across the area through tomorrow. Isolated diurnal \nshower or thunderstorm activity cannot be ruled out mainly across \nsouth MS, but coverage will be quite limited.\n\nWednesday through next weekend: By Wednesday, a backdoor front \nwill sink into the area from the northeast, then stall through \nmuch of the remainder of the week. While the front alone won't \npromise relief from the humid conditions for most of the area, \nparts of north and northeast MS will see lower, less oppressive \ndewpoints during part of Thursday and Friday. However, what might \nprove more impactful on conditions from Wednesday on will be \nincreasing chances/coverage of convection across our area. High \nprevalence of clouds/rain should help shave a few degrees off high\ntemps in many areas, helping to keep heat indices in check in \nmore areas. With upper flow becoming more northerly and \nnorthwesterly during this time frame, we will see potential for \none or two convective complexes moving into the area or developing\nover the area. However, by the end of the work week, an upper \nshortwave looks to become organized enough that an actual front \nwill develop across the Central Plains and push into the area \naround the late Friday/Saturday time frame. This will result in \ncontinued higher rain chances across the area heading into the \nweekend. It is uncertain how much progress this front will make \nthrough the area before stalling, but depending on the placement \nof the front, it will likely keeping higher than average rain \nchances around across much of the area.\n\nBefore clouds and rain begin to impact the heat, portions of \nsouth/west MS and northeast LA will likely still face high heat \nstress into the daytime Wednesday. Advisories or warnings may need\nto be extended for some portion of the area. /DL/",
